{
  "dom_id": "step-q345004",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212195/assets/a3623402e5d5df9339f1a3bb615c92ff5fcff9ae8d96c3630c5cb847a3038eda.png)\n\nWater is leaking out of a cone-shaped container so that the volume of the water decreases at a rate of $12\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$ The height of the container is three times its base radius. Find the rate of decrease of the radius of the water's surface when the radius is $6\\,\\text{in}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe radius is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} $\\text{in}/\\text{min}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's call $h$ the height of the conical volume of water and $r$ be the radius of its surface. Both of these quantities, as well as the water's volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-12\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=6\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we want the rate of change, which is $r.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $h$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $h$ in terms of $r.$\n\nWe're told that the height is always three times its base radius. So, we have $h=3r.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}(3r) \\\\  & =\\pi {r}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $r,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12 & =3\\pi (6)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -12 & =108\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{9\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the water's surface decreases at a rate of $\\frac{1}{9\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.$",
